course: biology
lesson: 279
title: Natural selection we can watch: moths, finches, germs and tuskless elephants
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Natural selection is usually slow, but sometimes we can watch it happen within a few years or decades, like a film on fast-forward.
explain: In industrial England, soot blackened the tree trunks, so pale peppered moths stood out and were eaten by birds while dark moths stayed hidden; dark moths became common near cities, and when the air was cleaned, pale moths came back.
explain: The same rule works everywhere: living things already differ a little, the place «chooses» who leaves more babies, and the next generation is a bit different.
explain: That is why your doctor says to finish the antibiotics: if you stop early, the toughest germs survive and multiply, and medicine slowly stops working on them.
explain: People can do the choosing too: by picking which dogs have puppies, we made every breed from a sausage dog to a Great Dane; that is artificial selection.
fact: In industrial England, soot darkened trees, so dark peppered moths were hidden while pale ones were eaten by birds; dark moths became common near cities, and pale moths came back when the air was cleaned.
fact: On the Galapagos Islands, finches have beaks of different shapes for seeds, insects or cactus; in a dry year, finches with bigger beaks survived because only hard seeds were left.
fact: Peter and Rosemary Grant measured finch beaks on the Galapagos for about 40 years.
fact: Antibiotics kill bacteria, but a few may be a bit tougher; if you stop early, the tough ones survive and multiply, and bacteria can become resistant, which is a real danger.
fact: When insects such as lice and mosquitoes are sprayed with poison, the survivors breed, and soon most of them carry the resistance; farmers change and mix methods to slow this down.
fact: After strong hurricanes in the Caribbean, scientists who had measured lizards just before by chance found that survivors had bigger toe pads that grip better, and the next generation had bigger toe pads too.
fact: City animals often become bolder than country ones, because bold ones get more food near people; some city birds even sing at a higher pitch to be heard over traffic.
fact: In Mozambique during a long war, poachers killed elephants with tusks; tuskless females survived more often, and within a few decades many more elephants were born without tusks.
fact: Dogs come from wolves, and people chose which dogs had puppies and over many generations made the breeds; this is artificial selection, Darwin's favourite example along with pigeons.
fact: From one wild cabbage plant, farmers bred cabbage, broccoli, cauliflower, kale and Brussels sprouts, choosing leaves, flowers or buds; they are all the same species.
fact: Most mammals stop digesting milk after babyhood, but in places where people kept cows for thousands of years, a change that keeps milk digestion spread; it is a recent example of human evolution, and grown-ups without the change, who get tummy trouble from milk, are called lactose intolerant.
fact: Birds ate the stick insects that looked least like twigs, so over many generations the survivors looked more and more like twigs; camouflage is a classic result of natural selection.
fact: Flu viruses change quickly, and the kinds the body does not recognise spread more, so flu vaccines are updated each year; this is evolution in months, not millions of years.
exercise: Why did dark peppered moths become common near English cities? | Soot made trees dark, so dark moths were hidden from birds | The smoke from the factories painted the moths black over time | Dark moths liked the warm factories | Pale moths flew away to the seaside
exercise: What happened to the moths when the air was cleaned? | Pale moths became common again | All the moths in the town died out | The moths slowly turned green like leaves | Dark moths became even more common
exercise: Why did Galapagos finches with bigger beaks survive a dry year? | Only hard seeds were left, and big beaks could crack them | Big beaks held more water, like a bottle, for the dry days | Big-beaked finches flew to another island | Big beaks scared the other birds away
exercise: Who measured finch beaks on the Galapagos for about 40 years? | Peter and Rosemary Grant | Charles Darwin and his son Francis | Jane Goodall and David Attenborough | Alfred Wallace and Henry Bates
exercise: Why should you finish a course of antibiotics? | Stopping early lets the toughest germs survive and multiply | The last few pills in the box are the strongest and taste the best | Antibiotics are wasted if left in the box | Germs get angry if you stop
exercise: What happens when farmers spray the same insect poison for years? | The surviving insects breed, and most become resistant | The insects all die out for ever, so spraying can stop | The insects learn to read the label | The poison grows stronger on its own each year
exercise: Which lizards survived the hurricanes in the Caribbean best? | Those with bigger, grippier toe pads | Those with the longest, heaviest tails to hold them | Those that were the brightest colour of all | Those that could swim fastest to safety
exercise: Why do some city birds sing at a higher pitch? | To be heard over traffic noise | Because city air is much thinner | Because they eat sweet food from bins | To copy car alarms just for fun
exercise: Why are more elephants in Mozambique now born without tusks? | Poachers killed tusked elephants, so tuskless ones left more babies | Elephants chewed their own tusks off, and their babies were then born without | Tusks melt in hot weather | Zoos removed the tusks of baby elephants
exercise: What is artificial selection? | People choosing which animals or plants have young | Animals choosing their own food | Robots making new animals | Nature choosing who survives a hurricane or a storm
exercise: Which vegetables were all bred from wild cabbage? | Broccoli, cauliflower, kale and Brussels sprouts | Carrots, potatoes, onions and peas | Tomatoes, peppers, beans and corn | Apples, pears, plums and cherries
exercise: Why can some adults drink milk easily? | People whose ancestors kept cows often carry a change that keeps milk digestion | Drinking lots of milk as a child trains the stomach to digest milk for ever after | Milk is easy for every adult mammal | Adults who like cheese can always drink milk
exercise: How did stick insects come to look like twigs? | Birds ate the ones that looked least like twigs | They glued bits of bark onto their bodies with spit | They stood very still in trees until they slowly turned to wood | Each one chose to look like a twig when it was born
exercise: Why is a new flu vaccine made each year? | Flu viruses change quickly, so the vaccine is updated | Last year's vaccine goes mouldy | Flu needs a new vaccine every year to stay alive and grow | Vaccines only work for one birthday
exercise: Which is natural selection and not artificial selection? | Hurricanes leaving lizards with better-gripping toes | Farmers breeding bigger cauliflowers | Dog breeders making sausage dogs | Pigeon fanciers breeding fancy tails
test: «doc gave me antibiotics, I feel fine after 2 days so I'll bin the rest» What is best? | Finish the course as the doctor says, so the toughest germs do not survive | Bin them, because feeling fine means every single germ is already dead and gone | Give the rest to your dog | Take them all at once tomorrow
test: «a chihuahua and a wolf, no way same family» What is right? | People bred all dogs from wolves by choosing which dogs had puppies | Chihuahuas come from mice that people kept as pets and slowly grew bigger | Chihuahuas are a kind of fox | Wolves came from chihuahuas
test: «broccoli and cabbage are totally different plants mate» What is right? | They were bred from the same wild cabbage and are the same species | Broccoli is a kind of small tree that farmers cut down while it is young | Broccoli grows from potato seeds | Cabbage is a kind of lettuce
test: Count them: 4 elephants walk by, and 2 of them have no tusks. Each elephant with tusks has 2. How many tusks, trunks and tails are there? | 4 tusks, 4 trunks and 4 tails | 8 tusks, 4 trunks and 4 tails | 4 tusks, 2 trunks and 4 tails | 2 tusks, 4 trunks and 2 tails
test: What would happen if all the trees near a city became pale again after the soot was cleaned? | Pale moths would be hidden and become common again | Dark moths would turn pale overnight | All moths would leave the city | Birds would stop eating moths
test: «the elephants decided to stop growing tusks so poachers leave 'em alone, smart» What is right? | No, elephants did not decide; tuskless ones survived more and had more babies | Yes, elephants chose to drop their tusks, and they taught their babies to do it too | Yes, they talked to each other about it | No, the tusks were taken by zoos
test: In a cartoon, a mosquito puts on a gas mask and laughs at the bug spray. What is really happening when mosquitoes resist spray? | A few already tough mosquitoes survive and breed until most are resistant | Each mosquito learns to hold its breath for longer every time it is sprayed | Mosquitoes wear tiny masks | The spray turns into food
test: Count them: a stick insect has 6 legs. How many legs do 5 stick insects on a twig have? | 30 | 25 | 20 | 11
test: «why do I have to get a flu jab every year, it's the same flu innit» What is right? | No, flu viruses change quickly, so the vaccine is updated | Yes, but the jab wears off after a week | Yes, it is the same flu, the jab is just a habit | No, flu turns into a cold each summer
test: A dry year leaves only big, hard seeds on an island. What would happen to the next generation of finches? | They would tend to have bigger beaks | They would tend to have smaller beaks | They would all stop eating seeds at once | They would grow teeth to crack the seeds
test: «my mate's lactose intolerant, means he's ill» What is closer to the truth? | Most mammals stop digesting milk after babyhood, so this is normal for many people | It means he has caught a dangerous germ from milk and needs to stay in bed till he is better | It means he drank too much milk as a baby | It means he is allergic to cows
test: Count them: a scientist catches 3 lizards before a storm and 2 after. Each lizard has 4 feet. How many feet did she measure in all? | 20 | 12 | 8 | 16
exam: Identify the principal cause of the increase in dark peppered moths near industrial cities. | Dark individuals were concealed upon soot-darkened trees and were less frequently eaten by birds | Factory smoke chemically altered the colour of individual moths | Pale moths migrated to the countryside of their own accord | Dark moths were deliberately bred by naturalists
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the premature discontinuation of a course of antibiotics. | It permits the more resistant bacteria to survive and multiply | It invariably eliminates all bacteria present within the body at once | It strengthens the antibiotic for future use | It has no consequence of any kind
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises artificial selection? | The deliberate choice by people of which organisms shall reproduce | The elimination of organisms by natural catastrophes | The choice by animals of their own diet | The spontaneous change of organisms by their own will
exam: Ascertain the reason for which influenza vaccines are revised annually. | The influenza virus changes rapidly, rendering earlier vaccines less effective | Vaccines deteriorate in storage within one month | The human body ceases to accept vaccines after one year | Influenza is replaced each year by the common cold
